WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.420
Every day, your kidneys filter about 180 litres of plasma.

2
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.200
Yet you pass only one or two litres of urine.

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.560
Why would an organ filter so much more than it lets go?

4
00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:22.223
The kidney's working unit is the nephron: a filter attached to a long tube.

5
00:00:23.503 --> 00:00:28.543
Blood enters a knot of capillaries, the glomerulus, through the afferent arteriole.

6
00:00:29.403 --> 00:00:31.623
It leaves through the efferent arteriole.

7
00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:37.493
Blood pressure pushes plasma through a barrier into Bowman's capsule, the cup around

8
00:00:37.493 --> 00:00:41.133
the capillaries. This fluid is the filtrate.

9
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:47.293
It carries water, salt, glucose and urea, but no blood cells and very little

10
00:00:47.293 --> 00:00:48.293
protein.

11
00:00:48.917 --> 00:00:51.277
From here on, the tube does two jobs.

12
00:00:52.477 --> 00:00:54.477
It takes substances back into the blood.

13
00:00:55.637 --> 00:00:56.837
That is reabsorption.

14
00:00:58.037 --> 00:01:00.257
It also adds substances from the blood.

15
00:01:01.437 --> 00:01:02.437
That is secretion.

16
00:01:03.797 --> 00:01:09.497
So keep a ledger. What leaves in urine is what was filtered, minus what was taken

17
00:01:09.497 --> 00:01:11.097
back, plus what was added.

18
00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.870
Run three substances through the same ledger.

19
00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:21.130
First, glucose. It is filtered at about its level in plasma.

20
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:27.090
The proximal tubule, the first stretch of tube, normally takes almost all of it

21
00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:30.150
back. Almost none is left to leave.

22
00:01:31.183 --> 00:01:37.743
Next, sodium. The proximal tubule takes back about 60 to 70% percent of the filtered

23
00:01:37.743 --> 00:01:40.063
sodium, and water follows it.

24
00:01:41.483 --> 00:01:43.303
Later stretches of the tube take back more.

25
00:01:44.603 --> 00:01:46.363
Only a small share is left to leave.

26
00:01:47.383 --> 00:01:53.763
Last, some drugs. They are filtered, and the proximal tubule also secretes them into

27
00:01:53.763 --> 00:01:57.783
the tube. So more of the drug can leave than was filtered.

28
00:01:58.917 --> 00:02:01.897
Now water, the biggest entry of all by volume.

29
00:02:03.177 --> 00:02:07.977
Of everything filtered each day, about 99% % comes back to the blood.

30
00:02:09.117 --> 00:02:11.617
So the filter does not set how much urine you make.

31
00:02:12.817 --> 00:02:13.817
The take-back does.

32
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:19.520
Suppose the tube took back 98% % of the water instead of 99%.

33
00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:24.160
Would urine rise by about 1%, % or by much more?

34
00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:26.460
Pause and decide.

35
00:02:32.883 --> 00:02:34.963
Much more: urine about doubles.

36
00:02:36.263 --> 00:02:38.543
What leaves is a thin sliver of a huge flow.

37
00:02:39.803 --> 00:02:42.983
Shift the take-back slightly, and that sliver changes a lot.

38
00:02:44.017 --> 00:02:46.817
A healthy kidney does not leave this to chance.

39
00:02:48.077 --> 00:02:52.417
When filtration rises, the proximal tubule takes back about the same share.

40
00:02:53.857 --> 00:02:57.957
Hormones make the fine adjustments in the last stretches, mainly the collecting

41
00:02:57.957 --> 00:02:58.957
duct.

42
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.440
So the kidney filters a lot, and the take-back largely sets how much water you lose.

43
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:11.140
Glucose, sodium, a drug and water all fit one ledger: filtered, minus taken back,

44
00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:11.840
plus added.
